  "result": "The command now prints **27.06** (it printed 26.09 before), and all 7 tests pass.\n\n**Root cause:** `to_cents` in `shop/money.py` padded the digits after the decimal point on the wrong side. `\"12.5\"` had its `\"5\"` padded to `\"05\"`, so 12.5 became 1205 cents instead of 1250. With quantity 2 and 8.25% tax that came out to 26.09 instead of 27.06.\n\n**Fix:** I changed `frac.rjust(2, \"0\")` to `frac.ljust(2, \"0\")`, so `\"5\"` becomes `\"50\"`. Inputs with two decimals, like `\"12.05\"`, and whole numbers, like `\"12\"`, still convert as before.\n\n**Tests:** pytest isn't installed, so I ran them with `python3 -m unittest`. No existing test covered `to_cents`, so I added `tests/test_money.py` with checks for whole numbers and for one and two decimals.\n\n**Separate bug, not fixed:** `to_cents` still gets negative amounts with decimals wrong. `\"-1.5\"` gives -50 instead of -150, because the cents are added to a negative whole-number part. The CLI doesn't seem to use negative prices, so I left it, but it's a one-line fix if you want it.",
